 This body of work falls under the umbrella of Quantitative Finance (specifically Statistical Arbitrage and Mathematical Finance / Financial Engineering), intersecting Stochastic Analysis, Econometrics, and Optimal Control Theory.
1. Stochastic Calculus & Continuous-Time Modeling
The mathematical foundation for modeling assets and spreads as continuous random processes over time.
 * Stochastic Differential Equations (SDEs): Itô calculus, Itô’s Lemma, and drift/diffusion mechanics.
 * Mean-Reverting Processes: Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU), Cox-Ingersoll-Ross (CIR), and Hull-White models.
 * Jump-Diffusion & Lévy Processes: Merton and Kou jump models to capture tail risk, structural shocks, and non-Gaussian returns.
 * Martingales & Change of Measure: Girsanov theorem and risk-neutral vs. physical probability measures (\mathbb{Q} vs. \mathbb{P}).
2. Time Series Econometrics & Statistical Testing
The tools used to test whether market data meets the structural conditions required by theoretical models.
 * Stationarity & Unit Root Tests: Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF), Phillips-Perron (PP), and KPSS tests.
 * Cointegration Analysis: Engle-Granger two-step method, Johansen eigenvalue test, and vector error correction models (VECM).
 * Memory & Long-Range Dependence: Hurst exponent (H), fractional Brownian motion, and variance ratio tests.
 * Regime Switching & Latent State Modeling: Hidden Markov Models (HMM) and Markov-Switching Autoregressive (MS-AR) frameworks.
3. State-Space Modeling & Signal Processing
Techniques for real-time parameter tracking when underlying market variables drift dynamically.
 * Linear Filtering: Discrete and continuous Kalman Filters for online parameter and hedge-ratio estimation.
 * Nonlinear & Non-Gaussian Filtering: Extended Kalman Filter (EKF), Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF), and Particle Filters (Sequential Monte Carlo).
 * Recursive Parameter Tracking: Dynamic OLS, exponential forgetting factors, and Bayesian parameter updates.
4. Stochastic Optimal Control & Decision Theory
The mathematical framework that dictates exactly when to buy, sell, or hold to maximize expected risk-adjusted returns.
 * Optimal Stopping Problems: Finding boundary thresholds for entering and exiting positions to extract maximum positive expected value (+EV).
 * Dynamic Programming: The Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation and viscosity solutions.
 * Portfolio & Position Sizing: Kelly Criterion, fractional Kelly, and mean-variance utility maximization.
5. Market Microstructure & Quantitative Execution
The study of exchange order mechanics, transaction frictions, and realistic price formation.
 * Optimal Execution & Liquidation: The Almgren-Chriss framework, temporary vs. permanent market impact, and transaction cost analysis (TCA).
 * Limit Order Book (LOB) Dynamics: Order flow toxicity (VPIN), queue priority, bid-ask spread decomposition, and adverse selection.
 * Market Making Models: Avellaneda-Stoikov inventory control framework.
6. Systematic Strategy Design & Risk Management
The practical architecture for combining models into live execution systems.
 * Statistical Arbitrage Strategies: Pairs trading, index/ETF arbitrage, cross-currency basis, and multi-asset basket cointegration.
 * Alpha Decay & Lifecycle Auditing: Tracking signal decay, model slippage, and performance regime shifts.
 * Tail-Risk & Capital Preservation: Value at Risk (VaR), Conditional VaR (Expected Shortfall), maximum drawdown constraints, and volatility targeting.


